Step two — declare the grain.

With the process chosen, the second step is to declare the grain — to state, in plain business terms, what one fact row represents — before you list a single dimension or measure. This is the same "most important decision" from module three, now sitting at its rightful place in the design sequence.

Say it as a sentence. For the sales-order process, the grain is: one row per product per order — that is, one order line. That's it — a sentence a business person would recognise. Not "one row per order", which hides the individual products. And not "one row per day", which is an aggregate. One order line.

And choose the finest grain the source allows. Prefer the lowest, most atomic level the data supports — here, the order line. Atomic grain is maximum flexibility: from order lines you can roll up to per-order, per-day, per-region, per-category totals — any summary you like, later. But you can never roll down. So capturing the detail now is exactly what keeps future questions answerable.

Why must this come before steps three and four? Because the grain is the contract those next two steps are held to. For dimensions, in step three: only things true at the order-line level can attach. For facts, in step four: only measures meaningful per order line belong. Declare the grain wrong, or vaguely, and steps three and four have nothing solid to test against. And the rule stands: one grain per fact table — never mix order lines and order totals in the same fact. So: step two declares the grain in one business sentence — "one order line" — at the finest level the source supports. It's the contract that makes identifying dimensions and facts possible. Fix it before going on.
